{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>And He removed the swarm.</b> Rashi explained: and they did not die as the frogs died, for had they died the Egyptians would have had benefit from the hides. And one should not ask why the frogs died and did not just go away like the swarm and the locusts, and one should not say: so that the land would become foul — for if so, why did the locusts not also die? The answer to this: that the Holy One, blessed be He, wished to teach that one who surrenders himself for the sanctification of the Name will be saved. Therefore all the frogs that did not go into the ovens died, and none survived except those that went into the ovens, as it is written (Exodus 8:9), ״וַיָּמֻתוּ הַצְפַרְדְּעִים מִן הַבָּתִּים מִן הַחֲצֵרוֹת וּמִן הַשָּׂדוֹת״ — meaning, but not those that were in the ovens, because they had surrendered themselves for the sanctification of the Name. And from here Hananiah, Mishael, and Azariah learned to surrender their lives for the sanctification of the Name. And we learn from here that one who surrenders himself for the sanctification of the Name will be saved, and one who does not surrender himself — the Holy One, blessed be He, has many agents to bring about his death from another place, as those frogs that did not go into the ovens died. And the swarm and the locusts went away, so that they would have no benefit from the hides, as for those that they salted from among them (Shemot Rabbah 13:7)."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:18:41.971505997Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}